#
# Rules to create the native Emu68 m68k target.
#

include $(SRCDIR)/config/aros.cfg

#
# AROS-<target> is the whole distribution, not just the ELF.
#
# It used to be declared as depending on kernel-link alone, which made
# `make AROS-emu68-m68k` a slow way of rebuilding the same ELF and left the
# distribution tree without S/, Fonts/, Storage/, Utilities/, Tools/ or
# AROS.boot. The SD card was therefore always made from a foreign tree, and
# every module on it -- libraries, Zune classes, the commands in C: -- came
# from there rather than from this build. A patch touching module code changed
# nothing that booted, silently. See AI_context/consolidated/history/ISSUE-0007.md.
#
# Shaped after arch/m68k-amiga, minus the Amiga-specific packaging and demos.
#
# compiler-posixc, compiler-stdcio and external-openurl-lib are named
# explicitly because they hang off the generic `AROS` metatarget rather than
# off AROS-<target>, so `workbench-complete workbench` does not reach them.
# Their absence is not a build error: the tree simply comes out with 59
# libraries instead of 62, and the boot stops waiting for posixc.library.
#
#MM- AROS-emu68-m68k : kernel-link-emu68-m68k software-emu68-m68k
#MM- AROS-emu68-m68k-quick : kernel-link-emu68-m68k-quick

# workbench-complete selects the platform USB stack through
# kernel-usb-$(AROS_TARGET_ARCH)-$(AROS_TARGET_CPU).  Binding that generic name
# to Poseidon's disk-based, non-PCI stack and Bellatrix's native DWC2 host
# driver is what puts USB on the card; without the alias a distribution
# silently contains neither side, which is what is wanted right now.
#
# USB IS DISABLED ON PURPOSE (2026-08-22).
#
# Two DWC2 drivers currently live side by side -- the inherited engine and the
# Bellatrix one replacing it file by file (ISSUE-0047) -- and the machine has
# open defects in the heap (ISSUE-0037) and in the first GL workload
# (ISSUE-0045). A host controller that owns interrupts and DMA is a large
# variable to leave in the picture while those are being chased, and none of
# them needs it.
#
# Re-enabling is uncommenting the line. It cannot be a make variable: mmake
# reads #MM lines as text when it scans the mmakefiles, so a $(USB_BACKEND)
# here would be a metatarget with that literal name, which does not exist and
# which mmake drops without a word.
#
# The Startup-Sequence needs no change. Its USB block is guarded on
# DEVS:USBHardware/dwc2emu68.device existing, and with this line commented out
# nothing installs it.
#
# MM- kernel-usb-m68k-emu68 : kernel-usb-nopci kernel-usb-dwc2emu68

# rom/ and workbench/ select the Bluetooth stack the same way, through
# kernel-bluetooth-$(AROS_TARGET_ARCH)-$(AROS_TARGET_CPU), and this target
# defined no such alias -- so AROS's Bluetooth has never been built here at
# all. That is what makes the bluzing submodule look necessary: the card
# carries a bluetooth.library from Extras/aros-bluzing because nothing was
# putting AROS's own there.
#
# AROS HEAD now ships the stack itself (rom/bluetooth, licensed
# LICENSE.aros-bluzing -- it is the same code, imported), so the alias is what
# turns it on. What is still missing afterwards is the transport: the stack
# talks to HCI devices in DEVS:Bluetooth, and the only one upstream is vbthci,
# a virtual one. The Pi's onboard controller hangs off the PL011 that
# soc/bluetooth/btuart.resource already owns, and nothing yet bridges the two.
# See ISSUE-0046.
#MM- kernel-bluetooth-m68k-emu68 : kernel-bluetooth kernel-bthciuart


#
# `contrib` is on the list because nothing else puts it there. Upstream expects
# the AROS contrib repository to be checked out into contrib/ and reached by an
# AROS-complete-<target> rule that this port does not have; ours is a symlink to
# aros/contrib in this repository, and its mmakefiles declare `#MM- contrib :`
# hooks that were being collected and never built. The symptom was a full build
# that produced a distribution with an Extras/ package missing its
# S/Package-Startup and its Prefs/Env-Archive/SYS/Packages registration, so the
# package existed on the card and never installed its assigns.
#
#MM- software-emu68-m68k : general-setup boot workbench-emu68-m68k \
#MM                         workbench-c-emu68-bootprogress \
#MM                         compiler-posixc compiler-stdcio external-openurl-lib \
#MM                         kernel-m68k-emu68-vcgfx hidd-vc4gallium contrib contrib-bellatrix
#MM- workbench-emu68-m68k : workbench-complete workbench

#MM- distfiles-emu68-m68k : AROS-emu68-m68k
#MM- distfiles-emu68-m68k-quick : AROS-emu68-m68k-quick
